{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2711","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2711","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2711\",\"parent_revision\":\"2ffc5e043490268913275a181672423606b2775b\",\"root_revision\":\"e80d6e77b90328d61bbbd21bac3bf7200fdb46686f29a77fc0d75a94c0d2a1dc\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe `cross_group` corroboration signal added to `reduce.py` (plugin-creator's shared ensemble-rule-review reducer) does not work \u2014 it is always `False` in production, regardless of how many distinct rule groups corroborate a finding.\\n\\nWhere observed: PR #2626 code review, `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py`.\\n\\nImpact: `reduce.py` is shared cross-plugin infrastructure consumed by multiple ensemble-review tools, not just DH. The cross-group corroboration signal was meant to elevate confidence for findings independently corroborated across 2+ different rule groups (a stronger signal than same-group corroboration alone). As shipped, it silently never fires \u2014 any consumer relying on `cross_group` to differentiate finding confidence gets no signal at all, with no error or warning.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:20:57.273985Z</sub>\\n\\n**Goal**: Fix reduce.py's cross_group corroboration signal, which is dead code that never fires in production due to the (group, location) dedup key, and restore the signal to work across 2+ distinct rule groups.\\n\\n**Assessed**: 2026-07-06T17:22:00Z\\n\\n**Conditions**:\\n\\n| # | Condition | State | Evidence or derivation path |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | reduce.py source exists and is accessible | AVAILABLE | plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py confirmed by fact-check verdict |\\n| 2 | cross_group field/property is non-functional or absent from Merged class | AVAILABLE | Fact-check: \\\"signal doesn't exist at all, was never implemented\\\" |\\n| 3 | Dedup key (group, location) prevents cross-group merging | AVAILABLE | Fact-check VERIFIED: \\\"structurally impossible due to (group, location) dedup key\\\"; impact-analyst confirmed key structure |\\n| 4 | cross_group signal intent is documented or inferable | DERIVABLE | Fact-check: UNVERIFIABLE for primary source; however PR #2626 description and issue #2711 description state the intent clearly enough for fix design |\\n| 5 | All code locations referencing cross_group in reduce.py are enumerated | DERIVABLE | grep --include=\\\"*.py\\\" -r \\\"cross_group\\\" plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/ |\\n| 6 | All consumer code accessing Merged.group or cross_group is enumerated | DERIVABLE | Impact-analyst identified: scorer.py (lines 390, 391, 403), test_reduce.py, orchestrator-playbook.md (pseudocode), focused-reviewer.md (instruction text), CI workflow. Complete enumeration via: grep -r \\\"\\\\.group\\\" examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py and git log --all --full-history --grep=\\\"cross_group\\\" |\\n| 7 | Current test coverage for cross-group corroboration is known | AVAILABLE | Fact-check via impact-analyst: zero coverage exists for cross-group behavior in test_reduce.py |\\n| 8 | Correct fix design (key mutation, groups: set[str] field, cross_group property) is validated against actual code | DERIVABLE | Read reduce.py lines containing the dedup logic, scorer.py interface expectations, and test_reduce.py to confirm proposed key change is compatible |\\n| 9 | Consumers (scorer.py line 390-403) will not break when Merged.group semantics change | DERIVABLE | Read examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py lines 390-403 to verify: (a) how m.group is used, (b) whether removal or rename breaks downstream code, (c) whether new groups: set[str] is compatible |\\n| 10 | orchestrator-playbook.md pseudocode that shows buggy dedup key is identified | DERIVABLE | Read orchestrator-playbook.md, search for (group, location) or dedup references; mark sections for update |\\n| 11 | focused-reviewer.md instruction sections about (group, location) key are identified | DERIVABLE | Read focused-reviewer.md, search for (group, location) or dedup semantics; mark sections for update |\\n| 12 | CI validation workflow that will enforce atomic fix (no dead code) is understood | DERIVABLE | Read .github/workflows/code-quality.yml; understand what dead-code detection or coverage enforcement will apply post-fix |\\n| 13 | Fix strategy does not introduce breaking changes to cross-plugin consumers | DERIVABLE | Cross-check scorer.py import statement and interface expectations against proposed key/groups/cross_group changes; verify no API breakage |\\n| 14 | Rollback and recovery strategy for the fix is defined | DERIVABLE | Determine: (a) is this a pure code change with no state migration? (b) are there existing instances of Merged with old key structure? (c) is migration needed? |\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 4, DERIVABLE 10, MISSING 0\\n\\n**Verdict**: READY\\n\\n**Rationale**:\\n\\nAll conditions are AVAILABLE or DERIVABLE. The fact-check verdict of UNVERIFIABLE for design intent does not block grooming because:\\n\\n1. The intent is clearly stated in PR #2626 description and issue #2711 description \u2014 both available as primary context.\\n2. The fix design does not depend on recovering a lost design document; it depends on understanding the current bug (dead code, never fires) and the desired behavior (corroborate across 2+ groups).\\n3. UNVERIFIABLE means \\\"no authoritative primary source found,\\\" not \\\"intent is unknown.\\\" The groomer can cite the issue and PR as sufficient evidence to write acceptance criteria.\\n\\nScope expansion from impact-analyst (scorer.py, orchestrator-playbook.md, focused-reviewer.md, CI workflow) is fully enumerated and each is DERIVABLE \u2014 readable from files or git history.\\n\\n**No reassessment changes from initial snapshot**: The Wave 1 fact-check and impact-analyst verdicts confirm the initial conditions. Condition 4 (design intent) remains DERIVABLE rather than MISSING because PR and issue context provides sufficient grounding.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **reduce.py's cross_group corroboration signal is dead code \u2014 never fires in production** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task - auto-derived from /code-review max --fix pr session, PR #2626\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-07-03\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-07-06)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:25:50.400831Z</sub>\\n\\n- Goal: Fix the cross_group corroboration signal in reduce.py so that findings from multiple rule groups at the same location are merged into a single entry with an observable cross_group indicator.\\n- Status: APPROVED\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - Root cause confirmed: dedup key `(finding.group, location)` at line 149 prevents cross-group aggregation \u2014 structurally impossible to produce cross_group = True\\n  - `Merged` dataclass fully inspectable at lines 66\u201379; no `cross_group` field present\\n  - Consumer (`scorer.py`) import path confirmed via `importlib`; access points identified at lines 390, 391, 403\\n  - Affected documentation and agent files identified: `orchestrator-playbook.md`, `focused-reviewer.md`\\n  - Fix locus confirmed: line 149 (key), lines 66\u201379 (dataclass), line 154 (group tracking)\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Test requirement: a test that passes two findings with different groups at the same location and asserts one merged entry with `cross_group = True` is derivable from the root cause description and the existing test file structure\\n  - Scorer compatibility: verifiable by reading `scorer.py` access points against the updated `Merged` contract before shipping\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:26:09.867074Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Bug fix\\n- Behavioral boundary: The observable contract of `reduce_findings()` \u2014 specifically whether two findings from different groups at the same normalized location produce one merged entry with `cross_group = True`, or two separate entries with no cross-group signal\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that cross-group corroboration is detectable at the `Merged` entry level and that the scorer can consume it correctly \u2014 not merely that the code was changed or that unrelated tests pass\\n</div>\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:22:25.035191Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Open `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py` and inspect the `Merged` dataclass (lines 66\u201379): observe there is no `cross_group` field defined anywhere on the class.\\n2. Read `reduce_findings()` at line 149: observe the deduplication key is `(finding.group, location)` \u2014 per-group isolation, not per-location aggregation.\\n3. Run the existing test suite: `uv run pytest tests/ -k reduce` \u2014 observe that no test exercises a scenario where two findings from different groups share the same normalized location.\\n4. Construct a synthetic call: pass two findings with different `.group` values but identical normalized locations into `reduce_findings()`. Observe that the function returns two separate `Merged` entries, each with a single group, rather than one merged entry with both groups recorded.\\n5. Confirm: no code path in `reduce.py` can ever set `cross_group = True` because the field does not exist and the dedup key prevents the aggregation that would produce it.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:22:45.404955Z</sub>\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 P1 (bug)\\n\\nThe feature is not partially degraded \u2014 it is structurally impossible to activate. The `Merged` dataclass has no `cross_group` field, and the dedup key `(group, location)` prevents the aggregation that would populate it. Any caller expecting cross-group confidence elevation receives silently wrong results: no error, no warning, no observable signal that the feature is non-functional. The bug is present in production as shipped.\\n\\nThe impact is confined to the ensemble-rule-review pipeline but the corroboration signal is the mechanism by which multi-group agreement elevates finding confidence \u2014 its absence means every finding is treated as single-group evidence regardless of how many independent rule groups flag the same location. This directly affects the quality of prioritization output the tool produces.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:23:08.158374Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Scoring pipeline produces incorrect confidence weights**: `scorer.py` (lines 390, 391, 403) accesses `m.group` on `Merged` entries expecting group-aware data, but because each entry is isolated to a single group by the dedup key, no entry ever reflects multi-group agreement. Confidence scores that should be elevated by corroboration are not.\\n- **Cross-group corroboration is structurally dead**: The `Merged` dataclass (lines 66\u201379 of `reduce.py`) has no `cross_group` field and no code path sets it. The feature has never functioned in production.\\n- **Documentation encodes the buggy algorithm**: `orchestrator-playbook.md` contains pseudocode showing the `(group, location)` key as the intended algorithm. Agents and engineers reading this are directed toward the wrong mental model.\\n- **Agent instructions reflect the broken design**: `plugins/plugin-creator/agents/focused-reviewer.md` instructs workers about the `(group, location)` key, propagating the incorrect understanding downstream.\\n- **Zero test coverage on the cross-group path**: No existing test exercises a scenario where two findings from different groups target the same location. The gap has gone undetected in CI.\\n- **Silent failure**: No runtime error, warning, or log distinguishes the broken state from a correctly functioning one.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:23:26.644538Z</sub>\\n\\n- Findings flagged independently by two or more rule groups at the same location will be correctly identified as corroborated and elevated in confidence \u2014 the primary value proposition of the ensemble review pipeline.\\n- The `Merged` dataclass will accurately reflect the full set of groups that contributed to each finding, enabling downstream scorers to weight multi-group agreement appropriately.\\n- Documentation (`orchestrator-playbook.md`) and agent instructions (`focused-reviewer.md`) will describe the algorithm as it actually works, removing a misleading mental model from the codebase.\\n- A new test for the cross-group path ensures regression detection \u2014 future changes cannot silently break the feature again.\\n- `scorer.py` compatibility will be verified, closing the gap between the reduce contract and its consumer.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:23:47.776824Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen `reduce_findings()` processes a batch of findings where two or more findings from different rule groups (e.g., \\\"style\\\" and \\\"security\\\") target the same normalized file location, it should:\\n\\n1. Produce exactly **one** `Merged` entry for that location \u2014 not one per group.\\n2. Record all contributing rule groups on that single entry.\\n3. Expose a `cross_group` property (or equivalent observable indicator) that is `True` when two or more distinct groups contributed, `False` when only one group contributed.\\n4. Preserve all other existing behavior for findings where only one group touches a given location.\\n\\nDownstream consumers (`scorer.py`) should be able to inspect `cross_group` on any `Merged` entry and apply elevated confidence weighting for corroborated findings without any change to their call sites beyond reading the new field.\\n\\nA finding from a single group at a location must have `cross_group = False`, ensuring the signal is specific to multi-group agreement and does not inflate all findings.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:24:10.469289Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given two findings with different `.group` values and the same normalized location, `reduce_findings()` returns exactly **one** `Merged` entry for that location \u2014 not two separate entries.\\n2. That `Merged` entry has an observable `cross_group` attribute that evaluates to `True` (or equivalent boolean indicator).\\n3. Given a finding where only one group touches a given location, the resulting `Merged` entry has `cross_group = False`.\\n4. A new test case exists that explicitly exercises the cross-group path: it constructs two findings with different groups at the same location, calls `reduce_findings()`, asserts one merged entry is returned, and asserts `cross_group is True` on that entry. This test must fail against the pre-fix code.\\n5. The existing test suite (`uv run pytest tests/ -k reduce`) passes without regressions after the fix.\\n6. `scorer.py` produces correct output when consuming the updated `Merged` entries \u2014 verified by running the scorer against a fixture containing a cross-group finding and confirming no `AttributeError`, `KeyError`, or changed output shape at the access points (lines 390, 391, 403).\\n7. `orchestrator-playbook.md` no longer documents `(group, location)` as the deduplication key; the updated pseudocode matches the actual post-fix algorithm.\\n8. CI passes (`uv run pytest` + type checks) with no new failures.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:24:51.652656Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Source | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py` lines 66\u201379 \u2014 `Merged` dataclass definition (no `cross_group` field) |\\n| Source | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py` line 149 \u2014 dedup key `(finding.group, location)` \u2014 root cause |\\n| Source | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py` line 154 \u2014 group tracking call that needs `groups.add(finding.group)` |\\n| Source | `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py` lines 390, 391, 403 \u2014 `m.group` access points; primary consumer of `Merged` |\\n| Docs | `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/orchestrator-playbook.md` \u2014 documents the buggy `(group, location)` algorithm in pseudocode |\\n| Agent | `plugins/plugin-creator/agents/focused-reviewer.md` \u2014 propagates the incorrect dedup key to worker agents |\\n| Skill | `/plugin-creator:ensemble-rule-review` \u2014 owning skill for this pipeline |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:24:30.922974Z</sub>\\n\\nPrimary fix targets:\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py` \u2014 root cause: `Merged` dataclass (lines 66\u201379), dedup key (line 149), group tracking (line 154)\\n\\nConsumer requiring compatibility verification:\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py` \u2014 accesses `m.group` at lines 390, 391, 403; must be verified against the updated `Merged` contract\\n\\nTest coverage gap:\\n- `tests/test_reduce.py` (or equivalent) \u2014 must receive a new test exercising the cross-group path\\n\\nDocumentation encoding the buggy algorithm:\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/orchestrator-playbook.md` \u2014 pseudocode documents `(group, location)` key as intended; must be updated to reflect the fixed algorithm\\n\\nAgent instructions reflecting the broken design:\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/agents/focused-reviewer.md` \u2014 instructs workers about `(group, location)` dedup; must be updated after fix\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:25:08.984060Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 the fix is self-contained within the ensemble-rule-review plugin and its shared test infrastructure. No prior work item must complete first.\\n- Blocks: Any downstream work that relies on correct cross-group confidence scoring in the scorer pipeline. If a follow-up item exists to tune confidence thresholds or weight multi-group signals, it cannot produce valid results until this fix is in place.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:25:27.946527Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The root cause is localized to three lines in `reduce.py` (dataclass definition, dedup key, group tracking). However, the fix touches five files: the primary source, its consumer (`scorer.py`) for compatibility verification, the test file for a new cross-group test, and two documentation/agent files that encode the broken algorithm. The scorer compatibility check at lines 390\u2013391 and 403 requires reading both the updated contract and the consumer logic to confirm no silent breakage. Writing a test that demonstrably fails pre-fix and passes post-fix adds modest but non-trivial effort. Documentation and agent file updates are straightforward once the algorithm is confirmed correct.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/agents/focused-reviewer.md` \u2014 line 58 instructs workers: \\\"The reducer keys on `(group, location)`, so two workers corroborate a shared line even when their `rule` slugs differ \u2014 provided both emit the same group id for that rule.\\\" After the fix, the corroboration key is `location`-only \u2014 different group ids CAN corroborate. This instruction currently causes agents to believe cross-group corroboration is impossible, which matches the bug behavior. After the fix, the instruction is wrong: it still says corroboration requires the same group id | Risk: HIGH | Why: this is a live agent prompt; focused-reviewer workers read this and use it to reason about how to emit findings; an agent following the stale instruction would continue to emit findings correctly (group ids are still needed per-rule for correct output), but an agent INTERPRETING the reducer's output would have a wrong mental model of what corroboration means | pattern: 'keys on.*group.*location'\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py::score_arm_b` \u2014 imports `reduce_findings` from `reduce.py` via `importlib` (lines 46\u201387); accesses `m.group` at lines 390, 391, and 403 inside set comprehensions and dict comprehensions over `Merged` objects; if `Merged.group` is removed or renamed, all three accesses become `AttributeError` at call time (silent at import, loud at runtime) | Risk: HIGH | Why: hard dependency on `Merged.group` attribute in three distinct access sites; the importlib boundary means no import-time error \u2014 failure surfaces only when `score_arm_b` is called; type checkers will catch this only if `reduce.py` is properly type-annotated | pattern: 'm\\\\.group'\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py::reduce_findings` \u2014 thin wrapper that calls `_reduce.reduce_findings(reports, keep_threshold)` and returns the result as a list; docstring at line 140 says \\\"List of Merged finding objects with .group, .location, .weight\\\" \u2014 becomes stale if `.group` is superseded by `.groups` | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: interface contract documented in docstring; stale docstring causes callers to access wrong attribute name\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/scorer.py::reported_keys` \u2014 accesses `f.group` on `Finding` objects (line 256), not `Merged`; `Finding.group: str` is NOT the changed field \u2014 `Finding` remains correct | Risk: LOW | Why: `Finding` dataclass is unchanged by the fix; only `Merged` changes\\n\\n- `examples/solid-review-ab/runner/cli.py` \u2014 indirect consumer via `from runner.scorer import score_arm_b`; does not directly access `Merged` attributes; the `_PLAN_ENSEMBLE` path constant (lines 69\u201377) hardcodes the path to `reduce.py`'s sibling `plan_ensemble.py`, not `reduce.py` itself | Risk: LOW | Why: no direct `Merged` attribute access; runtime failure only propagates if `scorer.py` breaks\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/conftest.py` \u2014 adds the scripts directory to `sys.path` so `import reduce as r` resolves in `test_reduce.py`; mechanism is path manipulation only, no `reduce.py` API usage | Risk: LOW | Why: no semantic dependency on `reduce.py` internals\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/plan_ensemble.py` \u2014 co-located sibling script; orchestrates worker assignment and prints the recommended `--keep-threshold`; does NOT import `reduce.py`, produce `Merged` objects, or consume `reduce_findings` output | Risk: LOW | Why: no code dependency; only mentioned together in SKILL.md documentation\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py::reduce_findings` \u2014 dedup key `(group, location)` becomes `location`-only; `Merged` dataclass gains `groups: set[str]` and a `cross_group` property; docstring for `reduce_findings` says \\\"Dedup on (group, normalized-location)\\\" and becomes stale | Risk: LOW | Why: isolated to this file; the fix is the primary change target, well-scoped, and existing tests exercise most paths\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py::Merged` \u2014 dataclass interface change: `group: str` (single group) must coexist with or be superseded by `groups: set[str]` (all corroborating groups); `cross_group` property is absent from current dataclass | Risk: HIGH | Why: three downstream consumers access `m.group` directly at runtime; breaking this field causes `AttributeError` in `scorer.py`, and the type-checkers (`ty check`, `basedpyright`) will fail CI on any mismatch\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/scripts/reduce.py::format_report` \u2014 output line format includes `group=<value>` sourced from `Merged.group`; if field is renamed, output format changes and any script parsing the reducer's printed output would break | Risk: LOW | Why: no consumer was found that programmatically parses `format_report` text output\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml::test-python` \u2014 runs `uv run -q pytest` without test path restriction; this discovers and runs both `test_reduce.py` and `examples/solid-review-ab/tests/test_scorer*.py`; if `Merged.group` is removed and `scorer.py` is not updated simultaneously, this job fails on the fix PR | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: CI is the enforcement gate \u2014 it will catch mismatched changes; the risk is that a partial fix (reduce.py only) triggers a CI failure that blocks the PR until scorer.py is also updated\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml::typecheck-ty` \u2014 runs `uv run -q ty check .`; will flag `m.group` access as an unresolved attribute error if `Merged.group` is removed and `scorer.py` is not updated | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: same simultaneity constraint as pytest; type-checking operates on both reduce.py and scorer.py in a single pass\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml::typecheck-python` \u2014 runs `basedpyright` via `pep723-loader`; same coverage as `ty` | Risk: LOW | Why: basedpyright and ty will agree; flagging it separately only for completeness\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/references/orchestrator-playbook.md` \u2014 contains literal pseudocode at the \\\"The reducer algorithm\\\" section showing `key = (f[\\\"group\\\"], normalize(f[\\\"location\\\"]))` as the correct dedup key; this is the exact bug being fixed; after the fix this pseudocode actively teaches the wrong algorithm to anyone implementing a reducer | Risk: HIGH | Why: the pseudocode is presented as a correct reference implementation \u2014 it encodes the bug; any future implementer or orchestrator agent copying it reproduces the defect | pattern: 'key = \\\\(f\\\\[\\\"group\\\"\\\\]'\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 line 291 describes the reducer as corroboration-weighting on `(group, location)` (\\\"corroboration-weight on `(group, location)`, drop the tail, and rank\\\"); this description becomes inaccurate after the fix; the `cross_group` signal that the fix enables is also not mentioned anywhere in this file | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: SKILL.md is AI-facing \u2014 an orchestrator reading it gets an incorrect description of what the reducer does and has no awareness of the `cross_group` differentiation signal\\n\\n- `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/ensemble-rule-review/references/measuring-success.md` \u2014 documents ensemble success metrics; may reference corroboration behavior; checked and found no explicit mention of `(group, location)` keying | Risk: LOW | Why: no direct keying description found\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible (`reduce.py` \u2014 primary change target)\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible (`scorer.py` critical \u2014 must be updated if `Merged.group` is removed; `cli.py` verify)\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added (`test_reduce.py` \u2014 add cross-group and `cross_group` property tests; `test_scorer.py`, `test_scorer_independent.py` \u2014 verify compatible with scorer changes)\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated (`orchestrator-playbook.md` pseudocode, `SKILL.md` reducer description)\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated (`code-quality.yml` \u2014 runs pytest and type checks; verify CI passes on complete change set)\\n- [ ] Every 